<h2 id="different">What Makes This PRP Different</h2>
<p>Platelet-rich plasma takes a different approach to musculoskeletal injury than surgery or steroid injections — it works with the body's own healing response instead of overriding it.</p>

<h3>Regenerative, not palliative</h3>
<p>Steroid injections reduce inflammation and mask pain, but they don't repair damaged tissue — and repeated use can weaken cartilage, tendons, and ligaments over time. PRP concentrates a patient's own platelets and growth factors and reintroduces them directly into the injured area to stimulate the body's natural repair process at the cellular level. It addresses the underlying injury, not just the symptom.</p>

<h3>Autologous</h3>
<p>Because it's derived from the patient's own blood, there's no foreign material and no risk of rejection — with a minimal side-effect profile compared to injected corticosteroids or synthetic alternatives.</p>

<h3>Non-surgical and in-office</h3>
<p>For many soft-tissue and joint injuries, PRP offers a non-surgical alternative — no incisions, no anesthesia risk, no lengthy post-op recovery. It's performed in-office in under an hour, and most patients resume normal activity within a day or two.</p>

<h3>Precision-driven</h3>
<p>Ultrasound guidance places the injection exactly at the site of injury rather than relying on surface landmarks — which matters both for clinical outcomes and for the accuracy of the documentation behind them.</p>

<h2 id="consistent">One Consistent Protocol</h2>
<p>Every case follows the same standardized preparation and technique. That consistency is the point: consistent inputs are what make outcomes — and the records behind them — consistent. It's the opposite of a bedside kit and a technique that changes with whoever happens to be in the room.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-label">Why standardization matters</div>
  <p class="callout-body">A protocol built around one defined preparation, ultrasound-guided delivery, and validated outcome measures produces records that are more accurate and more acceptable — to payers, to referring physicians, and to a practice tracking its own results.</p>
</div>

